	•	Section 2 Comments/Quotes: The overall cohort contained 4,464 potential DCD donors for whom withdrawal of life-sustaining therapy was initiated with intent for organ donation. Median age was 46 years rather than a reported mean, and 37% were female. Race was reported, and clinical history included diabetes, hypertension, cause and mechanism of death, neurologic reflexes, ventilatory variables, hemodynamics, laboratory values, and whether a lung offer had been made.

Section 3: Intervention & AI Methods
	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Retrospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: Gradient Boosting (XGBoost/LightGBM)
	•	Algorithm Name: Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), max variable model
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Donor metrics
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: Other
	•	Validation Method: Internal Split (Train/Test)
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: Yes (e.g., SHAP, LIME)
	•	Feature Selection Method: Automated (e.g., LASSO, Stepwise)
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: Not Reported
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: Separate XGBoost models were developed for 30- and 120-minute expiration using a 14-variable max-sample cohort and a 61-variable max-variable cohort. Each dataset was randomly divided 80:20 into training and held-out test sets. Multivariable logistic regression served as the baseline comparator. Predictors contributing less than 5% of the importance of the highest-ranked variable were removed and the model was rerun. Feature-importance analysis and an interactive probability calculator supported interpretation, but a specific hyperparameter-search procedure was not reported.

Section 4: AI Quality & Reproducibility (CONVINCE Standards)
	•	Missing Data Handling: Simple Imputation (Mean/Median)
	•	Class Imbalance Addressed: No
	•	Code Availability: Not Reported
	•	Data Preprocessing / Normalization Described: Yes
	•	Training Size (N): NR
	•	Test Size (N): NR
	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: Variables were required to have at least 85% completeness. Missing numerical values were imputed using medians and categorical values using the mode. The max-variable model used 2,092 donors and 61 predictors; the exact integer training and test counts resulting from the reported 80:20 random split were not stated. Expiration occurred in 59% and 82% of the max-variable cohort at 30 and 120 minutes, respectively, but no class weighting, resampling, or other explicit class-imbalance method was reported. A web application was provided, but source code or model weights were not reported.

